---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import samples from '../../data/samples.json';
const files=[{name:'Canon EOS R6 Mark II',sample:samples[0],mode:'Full-frame normal RAW (CR3)'},{name:'Sony A7 III',sample:samples[1],mode:'14-bit compressed RAW (ARW)'},{name:'Nikon D750',sample:samples[2],mode:'14-bit lossless compressed RAW (NEF)'}];
const format=(n:number)=>(n/1e6).toFixed(2);
const paths=[['/raw-file-size/','RAW file size'],['/compressed-vs-uncompressed-raw/','RAW compression'],['/raw-plus-jpeg/','RAW + JPEG']];
---
<Layout title="RAW + JPEG: When to Shoot Both & Storage Costs" description="Learn when RAW + JPEG is useful, how much additional storage to expect, and how to plan camera cards and backups with measured RAW samples."><article class="wrap prose"><p class="eyebrow">DUAL FORMAT WORKFLOW</p><h1>Should you shoot RAW + JPEG?</h1><p class="lead">RAW + JPEG saves an editable original and a ready-to-share rendering of each capture. It is useful when you need fast delivery today and the option to re-edit later, but it means more files to manage.</p><h2>When both formats make sense</h2><div class="scenario-grid"><article><h3>Events and portraits</h3><p>Send JPEG previews quickly while preserving RAW files for the final edit. Confirm your camera's naming and import workflow before the job.</p></article><article><h3>Travel</h3><p>Share JPEG images immediately and keep RAW files for selected photographs that deserve more attention later.</p></article><article><h3>Learning editing</h3><p>Compare the camera's JPEG decisions with your own development of the RAW original to understand color and tonal processing.</p></article></div><h2>How much storage does RAW + JPEG need?</h2><p>For each shot, add the separately saved JPEG file size to the RAW size. The examples below use three measured RAW originals and an <strong>illustrative 8 MB JPEG</strong>. These are projections, not measured RAW+JPEG pairs or guaranteed camera output sizes.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Camera</th><th>RAW (measured)</th><th>JPEG (assumed)</th><th>RAW + JPEG (estimated)</th></tr></thead><tbody>{files.map(f=><tr><th scope="row"><a href={`/${f.sample.slug}-raw-vs-jpeg/`}>{f.name}</a></th><td>{format(f.sample.rawBytes)} MB</td><td>8.00 MB</td><td>{format(f.sample.rawBytes+8e6)} MB</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><h2>What about two memory-card slots?</h2><p>On supported cameras, you may be able to record RAW on one card and JPEG on another, or write backups to both. Exact routing options depend on camera model and firmware. Do not treat split RAW/JPEG recording as a redundant backup: losing one card can still remove one format. For critical photos, use independent copies after import.</p><h2>Organize and back up your photographs</h2><p>Keep matching RAW and JPEG file names together during import. Decide whether JPEGs are temporary previews or part of your permanent archive. Verify the copied files before formatting cards, and maintain an independent backup rather than relying only on a second card in the camera.</p><h2>When is JPEG alone enough?</h2><p>Choose JPEG when speed, consistent in-camera color or low storage use is more important than extensive editing. RAW alone may be simpler if every image will be developed and exported later. The ideal format is a workflow choice, not a universal image-quality ranking.</p></article><section class="wrap prose compact"><h2>Related guides and tools</h2><ul>{paths.map(([href,label])=><li><a href={href}>{label}</a></li>)}</ul><p><a href="/cameras/">Measured camera index</a> · <a href="/memory-card-calculator/">Memory card calculator</a> · <a href="/methodology/">Sources and methods</a> · <a href="/">Compare RAW and JPEG</a></p></section></Layout>